/**
 * @page dev_streaming Streaming with SDR device
 * 
 * @if DOX_NAV
 * @ref dev_stream_config "Go to SDR device stream configuration topic"
 * @endif
 * 
 * @section streaming_summary Summary
 * 
 * Once the SDR device and stream parameters are configured, stream can be started using @ref lime::RFStream::Start() "Start()" function. This enables the aggregation of samples for Tx and Rx directions 
 * in the background. To stop the active stream and samples aggregation, call @ref lime::RFStream::Stop() "Stop()" function. Additionally, when the stream is active, you can view the current stream 
 * stats for Tx and Rx directions using @ref lime::RFStream::StreamStatus(lime::StreamStats*, lime::StreamStats*) "StreamStats()" function.
 * 
 * Once the stream is in an active state, samples of the required type can be written to Tx or read from Rx buffers. To receive buffered samples from Rx direction, call stream object member 
 * @ref lime::RFStream::Receive(lime::complex32f_t *const *, uint32_t, lime::StreamRxMeta *) "Receive()". To send samples through Tx direction, queue the samples to buffer by calling
 * stream object member @ref lime::RFStream::Transmit(const lime::complex32f_t *const *, uint32_t, const lime::StreamTxMeta *) "Transmit()".
 * 
 * @important Streaming interface is <b>not thread safe</b>. Avoid calling `Receive(...)` or `Transmit(...)` functions from multiple threads. More info in @ref important_stream_notes "Important notes" section.
 * 
 * @important Avoid mixing sample types when transmiting and retrieving samples via `Receive(...)` and `Transmit(...)` functions. Select the functions that accept the sample type that was 
 * specified via `format` parameter in stream configuration stage.
 *
 * @section generic_stream_example Generic stream example
 * 
 * Here is a generic code example for running a data stream in both directions in SISO mode:
 * 
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <iostream>
 * #include <vector>
 * #include <memory>
 * #include <utility>
 * #include <limesuiteng/DeviceRegistry.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/SDRDevice.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/RFStream.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/StreamConfig.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/DeviceHandle.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/SDRConfig.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/ToString.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/OpStatus.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/complex.h>
 * #include <limesuiteng/StreamMeta.h>
 * 
 * int main()
 * {
 *    ... // SDR device enumeration, registration and device/stream configuration 
 *    
 *    bool stop = false;
 *    bool send = false;
 *    bool endOfSamples = false;
 *    uint32_t bufferSize = samplesInPacket * packetsInTransac;
 *    uint32_t samplesRead = 0;
 *    uint32_t samplesSent = 0;
 *    lime::complex32f_t RxChABuf[bufferSize];
 *    lime::complex32f_t TxChABuf[bufferSize];
 *    lime::complex32f_t * const RxBuffers[2] = {RxChABuf, nullptr}; // Working only with channel A
 *    lime::complex32f_t * const TxBuffers[2] = {TxChABuf, nullptr}; // Working only with channel A
 *    lime::StreamStats RxStats, TxStats;
 *    lime::StreamRxMeta RxMeta;
 *    lime::StreamTxMeta TxMeta;
 *    TxMeta.hasTimestamp = true;
 * 
 *    ... // Other additional set up before starting the stream
 *    
 *    stream->Start()
 *    
 *    // Work until a stop condition (keyboard interrupt or any other interrupt) is met
 *    while(!stop)
 *    {
 *       samplesRead = stream->Receive(RxBuffers, bufferSize, &RxMeta);
 *       
 *       ... // Process the received samples 
 *    
 *       // Send response or any other data if necessary
 *       if(send)
 *       {
 *          ... // Fill TxChABuff with data 
 *          
 *          // Indicate the end of stream burst with the next transmission
 *          if(endOfSamples)
 *             TxMeta.flags = lime::StreamTxMeta::Flags::EndOfBurst;
 * 
 *          samplesSent = stream->Transmit(TxBuffers, bufferSize, &TxMeta);
 *       }
 * 
 *       // Stop, if the transmitter on the other end signals that the stream burst is finished
 *       stop = (RxMeta.flags & lime::StreamTxMeta::Flags::EndOfBurst) ? true : false;
 *    }
 *    stream->Stop();
 *    stream->StreamStatus(&RxStats, nullptr);
 *    std::cout << "Total quantity of received packets - " << RxStats.packets << std::endl;
 *    ... // Print out other stats
 * 
 *    ... // Other program code
 *    
 *    // End of a program
 *    stream->Stop();
 *    stream->Teardown();
 *    lime::DeviceRegistry::freeDevice(device);
 * }
 * @endcode
 * 
 * @subsection stream_buffers Work buffers
 * 
 * Before starting the stream, appropriate buffers for Rx and Tx directions must be defined. Since packets are managed internally and the user space operates using only samples, buffer sizes must also
 * be defined in sample quantity. Recommended minimum size for Rx or Tx direction buffers is the quantity of samples in a single packet (this depends on stream configuration). However, in order to 
 * maintain reasonable stream speeds, it is recommended to avoid the recommended minimum size and instead define the buffer sizes as a quantity of samples of multiple packets. In the example above
 * buffer size is calculated using stream parameters, which were set up before the stream start. Once the work buffers `RxChABuf` and `TxChABuf` of required size are defined, the starting addresses of 
 * the buffers are respectively stored in two dimensional arrays `RxBuffers` and `TxBuffers`. Two dimensional arrays are used to store the starting addresses of Rx and Tx buffers for different channels. 
 * By default the first argument must be the address of buffer for channel A and the second argument must be the address of buffer for channel B. If channel B is not required (operating in SISO mode), 
 * a `nullptr` can be passed as an argument. In the example above we are using a LimeSDR Mini V2, which operates only on channel A (channel B is not connected physically), therefore the second argument 
 * of `RxBuffers` and `TxBuffers` arrays is set to `nullptr`.
 * 
 * @if RST_SUPPORT
 * @embedRstVerbatim{
 * .. admonition:: Warning
 * 
 *    If operating in SISO mode, always use channel A for streaming. Setting the starting address of channel A buffer for Tx and Rx direction to `nullptr` will result in undefined behaviour.}
 * @else
 * @warning If operating in SISO mode, always use channel A for streaming. Setting the starting address of channel A buffer for Tx and Rx direction to `nullptr` will result in undefined behaviour.
 * @endif
 * 
 * @subsection stream_read Reading samples
 * 
 * Once the stream is started, aggregation of samples in an internal buffer for Rx direction starts (unless sample on PPS is enabled). In the example code above, once the stream is started, code
 * enters a worker loop, which reads out buffered data via `Receive(...)` function into work buffer `RxChABuf`. `Receive(...)` function call blocks until the work buffer (`RxChABuf`) is filled with 
 * samples or a timeout occurs. On reception, a timeout can occur if all of the internally buffered samples have been read and no new samples have been buffered. On return, function reports the quantity
 * of samples that were read from an internal buffer. `Receive(...)` function is also able to retrieve meta data about received samples. To retrieve the meta data, the address of a variable of `lime::StreamRxMeta` 
 * type must be passed as an argument for the third parameter of `Receive(...)` function. Consequently, `Receive(...)` function call updates the variable with the meta data of the first sample, that was read from 
 * an internal buffer. Meta data contains timestamps and stream flags. To check if incomming samples use timestamps, check if `hasTimestamp` is set to `true`. Additionally, meta data member `flags` can be used to
 * check stream specific flags. Flag `lime::StreamTxMeta::Flags::EndOfBurst` can be used to check for the end of stream burst. If meta data is not important, a `nullptr` argument can be passed to ommit the retrieval 
 * of packet meta data. If the size of a work buffer (in this case `RxChABuff`) is lesser than a size of a single packet in terms of sample quantity, only part of packet samples will be read out. The remaining part 
 * of samples is still stored and can simply be read out by calling the `Receive(...)` function again.
 * 
 * @subsection stream_write Writing samples
 * 
 * To send data in a worker loop, use `Transmit(...)` function. `Transmit(...)` function queues samples for transmission to an internally managed buffer. `Transmit(...)` function call always blocks until all
 * samples from work buffer (`TxChABuf`) are transferred to internally managed transmit buffer or a timeout occurs. On transmission, a timeout can occur if the internally managed transmit buffer is filled and 
 * has no empty space for new samples. On return, function reports the amount of samples that were queued for transmission. `Transmit(...)` function is also able to append meta data to the sample packets. To add 
 * meta data to sample packets, the address of a variable of `lime::StreamTxMeta` type must be passed as an argument for the third parameter of `Transmit(...)` function. Meta data contains timestamps and stream flags. 
 * To toggle the generation of timestamps for queued samples, meta data flag `hasTimestamp` must be set to value `true` or `false`. Generated timestamp type will match the type that was selected at stream parameter 
 * configuration stage. Additionally, meta data member `flags` can be used to set specific stream flags. Flag `lime::StreamTxMeta::Flags::EndOfBurst` can be used to indicate the end of stream burst. If the stream burst 
 * end flag is set and the last batch of samples queued for transmission do not make up a single packet, then the remaining samples slots in a packet are padded with value 0 and the samples are flushed out. If meta data 
 * is not important, a `nullptr` argument can be passed to ommit the generation of packet meta data. Only fully formed packets are added to transmit batch. If `Transmit(...)` function queues a quantity of samples that is 
 * lesser than a total sample quantity in a packet, the samples remain buffered until a sufficient amount of samples is added with the next `Transmit(...)` call to form a full packet (this does not apply for sample batch 
 * queues that have the end of stream burst flag enabled). 
 * 
 * @subsection stream_stats Monitoring statistics
 * 
 * It is possible to view the statistics of each stream direction. Stream direction statistics can be viewed during the stream and after the stream is stoped. To view stream statistics for both Tx and Rx directions, 
 * variables of `lime::StreamStats` type must be defined (`RxStats` and `TxStats` variables in the example code above). The addresses of defined variables must be passed as arguments to 
 * @ref lime::RFStream::StreamStatus(lime::StreamStats*, lime::StreamStats*) "StreamStats()" function parameter list. To avoid the retrieval of stream statistics for a particular direction, pass the `nullptr` argument.
 * In the example code above, statistics of Tx direction are ignored (because a `nullptr` argument is passed) and only the statistics of Rx direction are returned after the stream is stoped. 
 * Available statistics for a stream direction:
 * <ul>
 *    <li>Current timestamp</li>
 *    <li>Total amount of bytes transfered</li>
 *    <li>Data rate in B/s</li>
 *    <li>Total amount of packets transferred</li>
 *    <li>FIFO buffer status</li>
 *    <li>Data loss</li>
 *    <li>Data overruns</li>
 *    <li>Data underruns</li>
 * </ul>
 * 
 * To view the actual names of parameters view the definition of `lime::StreamStats` structure.
 * 
 * Streaming statistics are useful for observing the state of Rx and Tx sample queues. FIFO status allows to monitor the current capacity of sample queues for Rx and Tx directions. FIFO status allows to view:
 * <ul>
 *    <li>Total buffer capacity</li>
 *    <li>Occupied buffer capacity</li>
 *    <li>Ratio of occupied capacity to free capacity</li>
 * </ul>
 * 
 * The `overrun` parameter indicates the amount of times the software buffer overrun event occured. Software buffer overrun occurs, when the device receiver hardware produces more samples than the host
 * system software can process and the produced samples are dropped.  
 * The `underrun` parameter indicates the amount of times the Tx underrun event occured. Tx underrun occurs, when the sent data transmission timestamp is already behind the hardware's current timestamp.  
 * The `loss` parameter indicates the amount of times the receiver lost data. Data loss occurs, when the device receiver hardware produces malformed data.
 * 
 * @section important_stream_notes Important notes
 * 
 * <ul>
 *    <li>
 *       Streaming interface is <b>not thread safe</b> and multiple calls to `Receive(...)` or `Transmit(...)` from different threads can lead to poor performace or crashes. A single thread should manage a single
 *       stream function (eg. Receive with thread 1 and transmit with thread 2).
 *    </li>
 *    <li>
 *       It is possible to have more than one stream object with different stream parameter configurations for a single device, but it is recommended <b>to avoid running multiple streams simultaneously</b>.
 *    </li>
 *    <li>
 *       It is not possible to queue samples to Tx direction using `Transmit(...)` function, while the stream is inactive.
 *    </li>
 *    <li>
 *       Once stream is stopped, all receiver buffered data is deleted and cannot be retrieved using `Receive(...)` function. To properly end the stream session and read out all of the remaining data, utilize the
 *       stream flag `lime::StreamTxMeta::Flags::EndOfBurst`.
 *    </li>
 * </ul>
 * 
 */